BT 1 0 0 1 0 81.5 Tm /F3+0 11.5 Tf 15.5 TL (Article 19\(1\)\(a\) guarantees all citizens the right to freedom of speech and expression, while Article 19\(1\)\(b\)) Tj T* (guarantees the right to assemble peaceably and without arms. These provisions form the constitutional) Tj T* (foundation of political dissent in India. A protest allows citizens to communicate grievances collectively,) Tj T* (draw public attention to matters of concern and demand accountability from those exercising public power.) Tj T* (The right is particularly significant where individuals or groups lack access to other effective means of) Tj T* (influencing political decisions.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 44 368.8898 cm
q
BT 1 0 0 1 0 112.5 Tm 15.5 TL 1 1 1 rg /F3+0 11.5 Tf 15.5 TL (The Supreme Court has repeatedly recognised peaceful protest as an essential feature of a democratic) Tj T* (system. In ) Tj /F4+0 11.5 Tf 15.5 TL (Ramlila Maidan Incident v. Home Secretary, Union of India) Tj /F3+0 11.5 Tf 15.5 TL ( \(2012\), the Court observed that the) Tj T* -0.084157 Tw (right to assemble and demonstrate peacefully is fundamental to democratic functioning, and that preventive) Tj T* 0 Tw (powers must be exercised with caution so that they do not frustrate the exercise of constitutional freedoms.) Tj T* (In ) Tj /F4+0 11.5 Tf 15.5 TL (Mazdoor Kisan Shakti Sangathan v. Union of India) Tj /F3+0 11.5 Tf 15.5 TL (\(2018\), the Court recognised the importance of) Tj T* (balancing the rights of protesters with the interests of residents and commuters, holding that peaceful) Tj T* (demonstrations may be regulated in a manner that allows the exercise of the right while preventing undue) Tj T* (inconvenience to others.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 44 253.3898 cm
q
1 1 1 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 97 Tm /F3+0 11.5 Tf 15.5 TL (The protection afforded by these provisions, however, is not absolute. Article 19\(2\) permits reasonable) Tj T* (restrictions on freedom of speech and expression in the interests of public order, among other) Tj T* (constitutionally specified grounds. Article 19\(3\) permits reasonable restrictions on peaceful assembly in the) Tj T* (interests of the sovereignty and integrity of India or public order. The distinction between these provisions is) Tj T* (important. A restriction on the content of a protester's speech must be examined under Article 19\(2\), while) Tj T* (a restriction on the assembly itself must satisfy Article 19\(3\). Although a demonstration may involve both) Tj T* (rights simultaneously, the constitutional justification for restricting each must be assessed accordingly.) Tj T* ET
