BT 1 0 0 1 0 66 Tm /F3+0 11.5 Tf 15.5 TL (The National Capital Territory of Delhi occupies a distinct position within India's federal structure. Article) Tj T* (239AA provides for an elected Legislative Assembly and a Council of Ministers, while excluding the subjects) Tj T* (of public order, police and land from the Delhi Legislative Assembly's legislative competence. Consequently,) Tj T* (the Delhi Police functions under the Union Ministry of Home Affairs rather than the elected Delhi) Tj T* (Government.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 44 539.3898 cm
q
1 1 1 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 66 Tm /F3+0 11.5 Tf 15.5 TL (This arrangement creates a particular accountability question in the context of protest regulation. The) Tj T* (elected government may be responsible for several aspects of local governance, while the authority) Tj T* -0.103414 Tw (responsible for policing assemblies and imposing restrictions lies with the Union Government. The division of) Tj T* 0 Tw (responsibility can complicate the identification of the institution answerable for decisions concerning) Tj T* (prohibitory orders, detentions and the use of force.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 44 454.8898 cm
q
1 1 1 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 66 Tm /F3+0 11.5 Tf 15.5 TL (The constitutional position does not remove the obligation of the police or executive authorities to comply) Tj T* (with fundamental rights. Actions taken in the interests of public order remain subject to judicial review. The) Tj T* (question for this committee is whether the existing institutional arrangement provides adequate) Tj T* (mechanisms for democratic accountability, particularly where restrictions upon peaceful protest are) Tj T* (imposed in the national capital.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 44 426.8898 cm
q
1 1 1 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 4 Tm /F5+0 15 Tf 19 TL (Judicial Review and Constitutional Accountability) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 44 359.8898 cm
q
1 1 1 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 50.5 Tm /F3+0 11.5 Tf 15.5 TL (Articles 32 and 226 provide mechanisms through which individuals may approach the Supreme Court and) Tj T* (High Courts, respectively, for the enforcement of fundamental rights and other legal remedies. These) Tj T* (provisions are particularly important where executive orders restrict assemblies, where detention is alleged) Tj T* (to be unlawful or where the exercise of preventive powers is challenged as arbitrary.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 44 290.8898 cm
q
1 1 1 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 50.5 Tm /F3+0 11.5 Tf 15.5 TL (Judicial review serves as a check upon the exercise of executive discretion. It allows courts to examine) Tj T* (whether an order was issued by a competent authority, whether the statutory conditions were satisfied,) Tj T* (whether the restriction is supported by relevant material and whether it complies with constitutional) Tj T* (standards of reasonableness and proportionality.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 44 206.3898 cm
q
1 1 1 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 66 Tm /F3+0 11.5 Tf 15.5 TL (The constitutional framework therefore establishes a balance between the State's responsibility to maintain) Tj T* (public order and the citizen's right to express dissent. The existence of a statutory power to restrict an) Tj T* (assembly does not, by itself, establish the constitutional validity of every order issued under it. The manner) Tj T* (in which the power is exercised, the material upon which it is based and the extent of the restriction) Tj T* (imposed remain central to its legality.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 44 121.8898 cm
q
1 1 1 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 66 Tm /F3+0 11.5 Tf 15.5 TL (For the purposes of this agenda, the question is whether the existing framework adequately prevents the) Tj T* (misuse of preventive powers while allowing authorities to respond effectively to genuine threats to public) Tj T* (safety. Any proposed reform to the BNSS must therefore be examined not only in terms of its ability to) Tj T* (maintain public order, but also in terms of its compatibility with the constitutional freedoms and safeguards) Tj T* (that define the relationship between the State and its citizens.) Tj T* ET
