BT 1 0 0 1 0 153.5 Tm /F3+0 11.5 Tf 15 TL (The government's stated purpose in replacing the three colonial-era criminal statutes was to give India a) Tj T* (criminal justice system built around the Constitution and the citizen, with faster investigation and trial,) Tj T* (greater use of technology in summons, search and seizure, and stronger provision for victims. Features) Tj T* -0.075338 Tw (commonly cited in support of the BNSS include timelines for investigation and judgment, mandatory forensic) Tj T* 0 Tw (examination in serious offences, electronic registration of information and service of summons, and the) Tj T* (recording of searches by audio-visual means. Critics, including members of the Parliamentary Standing) Tj T* (Committee on Home Affairs who recorded dissent, argued that the changes expanded police discretion in) Tj T* (some respects, pointing for example to the rules on police custody under Section 187, which allow the) Tj T* (fifteen days of custody to be sought in parts across a longer period, and that several provisions were) Tj T* (retained from the old Code without addressing long-standing criticisms. Delegates should note that these) Tj T* (are positions in a continuing debate, not findings.) Tj T* ET
